A||||4||Formula88||Final Drive vs. Differential Ratio||||5||
Z||000000||Formula88||11-21-2001||07:27 PM||||Ok, got a question.  I know that a final drive is typically the total gear ratio through the transmission in top gear, i.e. the differential ratio x top gear ratio.<P>Are the Final Drive specs on Fiero trannies the actual final drive, or the ring & pinion ratio of the differential in the transaxle?<P>So, if a 4.10 Muncie has a 4th gear of .78 (don't know offhand, just guessing), does that mean it's got a final drive of 4.10 in 4th gear?<P>Top....Ring&...Final<BR>Gear...Pinion..Drive<BR>0.78 x 4.10 = 3.198 <BR>0.78 x 5.25 = 4.10<P>It was easier when you didn't have an overdrive.  The Final Drive was the same as the differential ratio, because you had a 1:1 high gear.   Which is it?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||11-21-2001||11:25 PM||||I am pretty sure the final drive ratio (as reported by GM) is just the equivalent of the "ring and pinion" ratio or "rear end ratio" or "axle ratio" that old cars had.  They now call it the "final drive," since it's a nice generic term (they don't look like axles anymore, nor are they necessarily in the rear end of the vehicle). <P>It's the ratio of the trans. output shaft's rpm to the differential carrier's rpm - both of which are inside the transaxle case and unavailable for comment  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I remember noting that my 1986 SE V6 4-speed ran about 2250rpm at 55mph (eyeballing the tach).  Its owner's manual reported the 4th gear ratio as 0.81 (overdrive), and the final drive ratio as 3.65.  Tires are about 24" outside diameter.  So you get:<P>2250rev/min* (1/(.81*3.65))* (24in*pi)* (ft/12in)* (mile/5280ft)* (60min/hour) = 54.3mph  Yeah, that works...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Notice I couldn't just say, "It's the axle ratio" and leave it at that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?  <P>Besides... a 3.65 "axle ratio" just sorta sounds right, doesn't it?    <P>      ||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||11-21-2001||11:34 PM||||I've always know it as "final" and "differential" being the same (pinion and ring) and "overall" being the input to output shaft ratio.||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||11-23-2001||12:33 PM||||Yeah, just like Terry said,also,tire diameter can change the final,or effective ratio as well.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-23-2001||05:43 PM||||A final drive is simply the final set of gears turning the diferential. In FWD transmissions this is nearly always a simple helical cut ring and pinion set.<P>There's 3 things involved when you are figuring gearing.<P>You can have a total ratio, that's the final and whatever gear you're in.<P>You've got a "simple" final ratio, which is simply the gear ratio of the ring and pinion in the diferential section of the transaxle.<P>Lastly you've got a calculated final ratio that is the "simple" final ratio adjusted by/for the tire size. I can't find the formula for it right now. This one tells you things like how much the speedometer will be off if you change the tire diameter. It also tells you how much gear advantage (power) you lose/gain depending on tire size changes.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
